1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,529
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,529 --> 00:00:09,795
This is chapter one: the JDK landscape, from Java 8 to Java 26.

3
00:00:10,245 --> 00:00:14,422
Which Java should we use has become a genuinely hard question.

4
00:00:14,422 --> 00:00:30,725
There are feature releases every six months, long-term-support releases every two years, a dozen vendor builds of the same source, and a licensing history confusing enough that many teams stayed on Java 8 for a decade rather than work it out.

5
00:00:31,625 --> 00:00:34,340
Three symptoms of the same confusion.

6
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:45,272
Version paralysis: a team still on Java 8 in 2026 is twelve years behind, missing virtual threads, records, pattern matching and a decade of GC work.

7
00:00:45,272 --> 00:00:55,471
And licence fear: Oracle changed its terms twice, and teams who read one announcement but not the next concluded that Java costs money now.

8
00:00:55,471 --> 00:00:57,673
For most builds, that's false.

9
00:00:58,123 --> 00:01:01,097
The third symptom arrives in production.

10
00:01:01,097 --> 00:01:03,253
UnsupportedClassVersionError.

11
00:01:03,253 --> 00:01:07,639
The service was built on Java 21 and deployed onto Java 17.

12
00:01:07,639 --> 00:01:15,595
The build passed, the tests passed, and the failure showed up at startup, in the environment that mattered.

13
00:01:16,495 --> 00:01:20,313
Since Java 9, the JDK ships on a release train.

14
00:01:20,763 --> 00:01:26,238
A feature release leaves every March and September, with whatever is ready.

15
00:01:26,238 --> 00:01:29,962
Features that aren't ready wait for the next train.

16
00:01:29,962 --> 00:01:38,065
Long-term-support releases come every two years since Java 17, which is why 21 and 25 are only two years apart.

17
00:01:38,515 --> 00:01:40,660
And here's the subtle part.

18
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:43,759
LTS is a vendor concept, not a JDK one.

19
00:01:43,759 --> 00:01:51,783
OpenJDK produces a release, and vendors choose which ones to support for years with backported fixes.

20
00:01:51,783 --> 00:01:57,345
The industry coordinates on the same ones, so LTS is real in practice.

21
00:01:57,345 --> 00:02:03,304
But no part of the JDK behaves differently because a release is called LTS.

22
00:02:04,204 --> 00:02:08,716
Every mainstream JDK is built from the same OpenJDK source.

23
00:02:08,716 --> 00:02:13,610
Temurin, Corretto, Microsoft's build, Zulu, Red Hat's, Oracle's.

24
00:02:13,610 --> 00:02:21,868
They differ in support duration, licence and platform coverage, not in the language or the standard library.

25
00:02:21,868 --> 00:02:25,921
A correct program behaves identically on all of them.

26
00:02:26,371 --> 00:02:30,394
So choose on support and licensing, not on capability.

27
00:02:30,394 --> 00:02:34,417
Corretto if you're on AWS, Microsoft's build on Azure.

28
00:02:34,417 --> 00:02:40,153
And for most teams, the honest answer is Temurin, and stop thinking about it.

29
00:02:41,053 --> 00:02:43,643
Here's the licensing history.

30
00:02:43,643 --> 00:02:48,199
Before 2019, Oracle's JDK was free for general use.

31
00:02:48,649 --> 00:02:55,959
In 2019, with Java 11, Oracle's own build started to need a paid subscription for production.

32
00:02:55,959 --> 00:02:59,103
That's the change that caused the alarm.

33
00:02:59,553 --> 00:03:07,524
In 2021, with Java 17, Oracle's build became free again under its no-fee terms, with conditions.

34
00:03:07,524 --> 00:03:13,419
That reversal got far less attention, which is why the belief persists.

35
00:03:13,869 --> 00:03:21,656
And throughout, every OpenJDK build has been free under GPL version two with the Classpath Exception.

36
00:03:22,106 --> 00:03:29,884
The Classpath Exception is the clause that lets your proprietary code link against the JDK without becoming GPL.

37
00:03:29,884 --> 00:03:33,703
So it's free, including in production and commercially.

38
00:03:33,703 --> 00:03:38,981
Oracle's own build is the only one where you need to read the current terms.

39
00:03:39,881 --> 00:03:47,282
Every class file records a major version, and the rule is simple: Java version plus forty-four.

40
00:03:47,282 --> 00:03:51,879
Fifty-two for Java 8, sixty-five for 21, sixty-nine for 25.

41
00:03:51,879 --> 00:03:57,645
A JVM refuses any class file with a higher major version than it supports.

42
00:03:57,645 --> 00:04:03,177
That's exactly the sixty-five versus sixty-one in our production error.

43
00:04:03,627 --> 00:04:09,615
To check the version at runtime, use Runtime dot version, and ask for the feature number.

44
00:04:09,615 --> 00:04:12,980
Don't parse the java dot version property by hand.

45
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:18,901
It changed format from one point eight to nine, and old build scripts still break on it.

46
00:04:19,801 --> 00:04:22,885
Targeting an older JDK has a classic trap.

47
00:04:22,885 --> 00:04:33,312
Minus source eight, minus target eight sets the language level and the bytecode version, but still compiles against the current JDK's library.

48
00:04:33,312 --> 00:04:39,407
So Stream dot toList compiles happily, then fails on Java 8 with NoSuchMethodError.

49
00:04:39,857 --> 00:04:42,042
The release flag fixes this.

50
00:04:42,042 --> 00:04:51,953
The JDK ships the historical API signatures of each release in a file called ct dot sym, and the compiler checks against those.

51
00:04:51,953 --> 00:04:55,153
The same mistake becomes a compile error.

52
00:04:55,603 --> 00:04:58,668
In Maven, that's the compiler release property.

53
00:04:58,668 --> 00:05:01,341
There's no reason to use the older flags.

54
00:05:02,241 --> 00:05:06,743
Declare the JDK instead of inheriting it from the machine.

55
00:05:06,743 --> 00:05:16,833
Gradle toolchains are the strongest option: Gradle downloads the specified JDK, so laptops and CI build with exactly the same one.

56
00:05:17,283 --> 00:05:20,427
In container images, pin the patch version.

57
00:05:20,427 --> 00:05:33,219
A floating tag like temurin twenty-five j-r-e resolves to whatever is current when the image is pulled, so the JDK can change between a passing CI run and a production deploy.

58
00:05:33,669 --> 00:05:38,170
Locally, SDKMAN installs and switches JDKs per shell.

59
00:05:38,170 --> 00:05:47,170
And form one habit: run java minus version and mvn minus version before debugging anything version-shaped.

60
00:05:47,170 --> 00:05:50,907
Maven often runs on a JDK you didn't expect.

61
00:05:51,807 --> 00:05:54,643
Which version should you be on?

62
00:05:54,643 --> 00:05:57,387
New services start on Java 25.

63
00:05:57,387 --> 00:06:01,961
From 11, go to 21, and from 17, go straight to 25.

64
00:06:02,411 --> 00:06:04,493
Java 8 deserves two steps.

65
00:06:04,493 --> 00:06:05,853
Land on 17 first.

66
00:06:05,853 --> 00:06:20,182
Going eight to twenty-five in one jump combines the module system's encapsulation, the removed Java EE modules, and a decade of library upgrades into one change nobody can bisect.

67
00:06:20,632 --> 00:06:26,607
And run the latest non-LTS release in CI, even if production stays on LTS.

68
00:06:26,607 --> 00:06:36,376
It surfaces deprecations and removals early, so the next LTS upgrade becomes a confirmation rather than an investigation.

69
00:06:37,276 --> 00:06:38,645
Production rules.

70
00:06:38,645 --> 00:06:41,222
Default to Java 25 for new work.

71
00:06:41,222 --> 00:06:43,478
Always use the release flag.

72
00:06:43,478 --> 00:06:50,243
Use a GPL with Classpath Exception build, Temurin unless something points elsewhere.

73
00:06:50,243 --> 00:06:52,498
Pin the exact patch version.

74
00:06:52,498 --> 00:06:55,478
Let toolchains declare the build JDK.

75
00:06:55,478 --> 00:06:58,780
And run the latest non-LTS release in CI.

76
00:06:59,230 --> 00:07:02,505
Never use minus source and minus target.

77
00:07:02,505 --> 00:07:09,545
Never run a non-LTS release in production without an automated six-month upgrade path.

78
00:07:09,545 --> 00:07:12,001
Never use floating image tags.

79
00:07:12,001 --> 00:07:16,094
Never assume Oracle's JDK has the OpenJDK licence.

80
00:07:16,094 --> 00:07:19,369
And never jump from 8 to 25 in one step.

81
00:07:20,269 --> 00:07:20,923
To recap.

82
00:07:20,923 --> 00:07:25,503
A feature release every six months, and an LTS every two years.

83
00:07:25,503 --> 00:07:32,628
Every mainstream JDK comes from the same source, so choose on support, and Temurin is the default.

84
00:07:32,628 --> 00:07:37,136
GPL with the Classpath Exception means free, commercially too.

85
00:07:37,136 --> 00:07:43,970
And class file major version is Java plus forty-four, so always compile with the release flag.

86
00:07:44,420 --> 00:07:47,784
Next is chapter two: the JVM runtime model.

87
00:07:47,784 --> 00:07:49,348
Thanks for watching.
